#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064143 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064306 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "44425e0c-c9f0-5589-a298-071f456fd0c5" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating X Raised to the Power of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "a369d9b2-7637-5339-bd75-5f49b1aac962" :question/math-academy-id "e-9842" :question/problem "Find the derivative of the function $y = x^{x^3}.$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny = x^{x^3}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{{x}^{3}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & ={x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =({x}^{3})^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+({x}^{3})\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =3{x}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $y'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=x^{x^3}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{{x}^{3}}(3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{{x}^{3}}({x}^{2}(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)) \\\\  & ={x}^{{x}^{3}+2}(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064144 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064307 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6cc92652-6746-583d-bb0e-53991d5f5e1d" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Function Raised to the Power of X"}] :question/id #uuid "bdccebc1-05df-5c7b-a57b-fa50df42a32e" :question/math-academy-id "e-9843" :question/problem "Find the derivative of the function $y = (x^2-1)^x.$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny = (x^2-1)^x.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}-1)^{x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-1)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-1)+x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-1)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-1)+x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-1}\\cdot 2x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-1)+\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-1)+\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y = (x^2-1)^x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =({x}^{2}-1)^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-1)+\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-1}) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-1)^{x}(\\frac{({x}^{2}-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-1)}{{x}^{2}-1}+\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-1}) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-1)^{x-1}(({x}^{2}-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-1)+2{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064145 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064308 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "94a2b8cb-aca4-5e18-bb5c-71c0738c9e30" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Function Raised to the Power of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "4d047c1a-308b-553b-89e4-9afc5cc9f766" :question/math-academy-id "e-9844" :question/problem "Find the derivative of the function $y = (\\sin x)^{\\cos x}.$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny = (\\sin x)^{\\cos x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x))+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $y'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y = (\\sin x)^{\\cos x}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x))\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504623 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064306 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "44425e0c-c9f0-5589-a298-071f456fd0c5" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating X Raised to the Power of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f344ed1-cd68-5ca8-8b22-83ca0921337f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504113} {:answer/id #uuid "32bbc90d-2767-5dfe-80db-b6e744066093" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504118} {:answer/id #uuid "3f1e446a-4d32-5156-985e-6ae51be667a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504143} {:answer/id #uuid "83ae2e03-d9e5-5eff-a83b-a5def56f354c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504261} {:answer/id #uuid "d3aa3ff5-94a6-5325-9b86-ac25ad8c1b82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504396}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f1e446a-4d32-5156-985e-6ae51be667a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504143} :answer-field/id #uuid "4723771a-307d-5638-bcc4-3cc4b09d18e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504507}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3fe57972-490a-52da-b89f-179c602b71c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-138749" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=6{x}^{x}.$ Its derivative is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=6{x}^{x}\\cdot g(x).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=6{x}^{x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6{x}^{x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡6+\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡6+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6)}^{′}+(x)^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+(x)\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =0+1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=6{x}^{x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =6{x}^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504618 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064306 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "44425e0c-c9f0-5589-a298-071f456fd0c5" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating X Raised to the Power of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16cac734-0892-5ff2-a1ff-c0fbd3626b86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10{x}^{2}+10x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504073} {:answer/id #uuid "6ba48682-04f1-5b7d-a7ef-91546e9c66ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10{x}^{2}+2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504221} {:answer/id #uuid "76897061-1d77-5f85-af3e-f9654624d9fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{x}^{2}+10\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504234} {:answer/id #uuid "c1cc2afd-bb0e-5601-811e-65ad382a5a7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{x}^{2}+10{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504363} {:answer/id #uuid "f73153f7-d5e1-5b7b-8f5c-b7bd0c550c73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{x}^{2}-2x+10x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504461}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1cc2afd-bb0e-5601-811e-65ad382a5a7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{x}^{2}+10{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504363} :answer-field/id #uuid "a560e212-9ade-5ff3-a8f4-f653183e4352" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504552}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "358ad886-8781-5867-add0-1dfbbd99e7fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-138791" :question/problem "Consider the function $y={x}^{5{x}^{2}-2}.$ Its derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{5{x}^{2}-3}\\cdot g(x).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny={x}^{5{x}^{2}-2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{5{x}^{2}-2}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =(5{x}^{2}-2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}((5{x}^{2}-2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{x}^{2}-2)\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+(5{x}^{2}-2)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =10x\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x+(5{x}^{2}-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =10x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{5{x}^{2}-2}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y(\\frac{5{x}^{2}-2}{x}+10x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y={x}^{5{x}^{2}-2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{5{x}^{2}-2}(\\frac{5{x}^{2}-2}{x}+10x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & ={x}^{5{x}^{2}-2}(\\frac{5{x}^{2}-2}{x}+\\frac{10{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}) \\\\  & ={x}^{5{x}^{2}-3}(5{x}^{2}+10{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=5{x}^{2}+10{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504612 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064307 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6cc92652-6746-583d-bb0e-53991d5f5e1d" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Function Raised to the Power of X"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a67a799f-2928-5adf-b9c5-d0bc276d7601" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-x)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504324} {:answer/id #uuid "aaf45454-7f81-5c74-be53-5259f10cc477" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504328} {:answer/id #uuid "b6075d2e-c34b-5d83-bb95-5c44a85e6863" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504346} {:answer/id #uuid "c8b4cfe8-7a15-5675-896e-7797cab89578" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-x)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504369} {:answer/id #uuid "d3556c8b-6cf8-5718-940d-e4fc62a213e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504392}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6075d2e-c34b-5d83-bb95-5c44a85e6863" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504346} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b94d114-e5cb-5a20-87db-9ae7838aedfe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504497}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "22063a8c-2673-5c08-b0b7-4fcd50355c5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-138809" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=(\\sqrt{x})^{x}.$ Its derivative is given by \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2}{(\\sqrt{x})}^{x}\\cdot g(x).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=(\\sqrt{x})^{x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\sqrt{x})^{x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{x})) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(x)^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{x}))+(x)\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{x}))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{x})+x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{x})+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{2}y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=(\\sqrt{x})^{x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{x})^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504641 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064307 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6cc92652-6746-583d-bb0e-53991d5f5e1d" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Function Raised to the Power of X"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "182d4fea-cab3-5c77-92d8-85665c78e002" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504074} {:answer/id #uuid "420d139d-afbe-5844-a8dd-8419647c77a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504145} {:answer/id #uuid "65ad5bd6-c5c9-54e5-bc74-3bf9d08af3ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504207} {:answer/id #uuid "86880236-33cf-5fe7-ad90-0ffd13b9f4a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504266} {:answer/id #uuid "d5c9c22c-95a4-5257-aadf-de4e6f6689bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504403}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65ad5bd6-c5c9-54e5-bc74-3bf9d08af3ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504207} :answer-field/id #uuid "49f97b4d-3e4e-5d71-8051-0c4a12624cb5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504508}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "73c29bd9-9c93-5235-9a81-b902322f8bb3" :question/math-academy-id "q-138837" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{x}.$ Its derivative is given by  \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{x}\\cdot g(x).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x))+(x)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504682 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064307 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6cc92652-6746-583d-bb0e-53991d5f5e1d" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Function Raised to the Power of X"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12032542-1f9c-57f6-90bc-70329c9ef372" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{2}+x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3x)+{x}^{2}-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504063} {:answer/id #uuid "3bcf7ad8-6ade-58fe-a3c7-26be7458e890" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{2}+3x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3x)+2{x}^{2}+3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504136} {:answer/id #uuid "40e1fd46-277e-5898-af07-c9c85bd000aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{2}-x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3x)+3{x}^{2}+2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504144} {:answer/id #uuid "a15daf0d-9d36-5b25-b77e-f17ceedee41a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3{x}^{2}+x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3x)+{x}^{2}+3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504320} {:answer/id #uuid "df98c2d6-7aea-57d0-915f-ebcd73c3427e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{2}+x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3x)+2{x}^{2}+x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504420}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3bcf7ad8-6ade-58fe-a3c7-26be7458e890" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{2}+3x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3x)+2{x}^{2}+3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504136} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8108104-8398-500c-9620-e32d585bdb8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504574}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cdcbfec1-c9f8-5550-af44-736f646a25ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-138840" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=({x}^{2}+3x)^{x}.$ Its derivative is given by \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=({x}^{2}+3x)^{x-1}\\cdot g(x).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=({x}^{2}+3x)^{x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+3x)^{x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3x))+(x)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3x)+x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+3x}\\cdot 2x+3) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3x)+\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3x}{{x}^{2}+3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3x)+\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3x}{{x}^{2}+3x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=({x}^{2}+3x)^{x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =({x}^{2}+3x)^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3x)+\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3x}{{x}^{2}+3x}) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+3x)^{x}(\\frac{({x}^{2}+3x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3x)}{{x}^{2}+3x}+\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3x}{{x}^{2}+3x}) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+3x)^{x-1}(({x}^{2}+3x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3x)+(2{x}^{2}+3x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $g(x)=({x}^{2}+3x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3x)+(2{x}^{2}+3x).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410419 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064308 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "94a2b8cb-aca4-5e18-bb5c-71c0738c9e30" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Function Raised to the Power of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11871c64-6368-59c5-9ec4-a19994f69e2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-x{tan}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410216} {:answer/id #uuid "543a59b8-c1fa-5f13-aee5-fec0cb00f970" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410262} {:answer/id #uuid "80147df6-2320-55c9-944a-766dbd01c6c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410284} {:answer/id #uuid "9c2b317b-6389-5a05-8b28-b44cfeff3161" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410304} {:answer/id #uuid "b4326574-8b69-5df3-9063-abf1241c8efd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+5x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410325}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80147df6-2320-55c9-944a-766dbd01c6c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410284} :answer-field/id #uuid "19581f53-386c-55c5-88ff-b5d2b3116bb1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410377}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "29625a08-0232-5ce8-97e1-f2bb9cf1a9d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-138856" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{5x}.$ Its derivative is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=5(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{5x}\\cdot g(x).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{5x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{5x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =5x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(5x)^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x))+(5x)\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =5\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+5x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-5x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{5x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =5(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{5x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504663 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064308 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "94a2b8cb-aca4-5e18-bb5c-71c0738c9e30" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Function Raised to the Power of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6fd19c25-5aab-572e-808f-2654216bf71a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504226} {:answer/id #uuid "7035e9b8-2a3c-533d-bcf2-3bfb073ccc28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504228} {:answer/id #uuid "8b404956-da94-552b-947d-159dfaef39dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504275} {:answer/id #uuid "958860f4-6bc6-5bdf-97ca-7bb1fc4df790" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504299} {:answer/id #uuid "a0be53dd-9f2c-5eb8-a506-84c246e69f71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504316}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "958860f4-6bc6-5bdf-97ca-7bb1fc4df790" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504299} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba9151a4-b3b8-572e-a482-a0acf45515d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504567}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a8502904-ea72-54be-8bde-7a62b1b58e76" :question/math-academy-id "q-138863" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{5x}$ at $x=e.$" :question/worked-solution "The slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=e$ equals the function's derivative at the corresponding point.\n\nWe have the function \n\n$$\ny=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{5x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{5x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =5x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(5x)^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x))+(5x)\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =5\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+5x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\frac{5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{5x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{5x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of $y=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{5x}$ at $x=e$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}{|}_{x=e} & =5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)^{5\\cdot e}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡e}) \\\\  & =5(1)^{5e}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+\\frac{1}{1}) \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064648 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064307 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6cc92652-6746-583d-bb0e-53991d5f5e1d" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Function Raised to the Power of X"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07651916-0973-556a-b9c6-a20002d3cbf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504044} {:answer/id #uuid "27f2940b-1ddc-59bd-aa4d-a19c8dfaf251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504101} {:answer/id #uuid "430fa093-d180-5fb7-8644-1b06f135536c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504150} {:answer/id #uuid "458cddab-14fc-5f26-9c92-145cd90d2727" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504156} {:answer/id #uuid "6d6767d3-90fd-55f9-99ca-5116679ff0b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504223}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "458cddab-14fc-5f26-9c92-145cd90d2727" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504156} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b873f0e-d503-59f0-bb98-854722b44541" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061945}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e49f218d-0bc2-5311-beb0-0c74453b8fc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-2519" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=(2x)^{x}.$ Its derivative is given by \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(2x)^{x}\\cdot g(x).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=(2x)^{x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((2x)^{x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(x)^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x))+(x)\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2x}\\cdot 2) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=y=(2x)^{x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(2x)^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504706 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064306 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "44425e0c-c9f0-5589-a298-071f456fd0c5" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating X Raised to the Power of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b8d60af-e149-5616-afdf-10d5747154fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-5x^2-10x^2\\ln x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504056}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b8d60af-e149-5616-afdf-10d5747154fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-5x^2-10x^2\\ln x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504056} :answer-field/id #uuid "834f15be-62e7-5d6a-9efc-72702ab37e17" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504537}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f33cce2a-3b3e-5ad7-89aa-ae8325e4e759" :question/math-academy-id "q-341711" :question/problem "Given that $y=e{x}^{2-5{x}^{2}}$, find ${y}^{′}.$\n\n${y}^{′}=e{x}^{1-5{x}^{2}}\\cdot ($\n{{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=e{x}^{2-5{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e{x}^{2-5{x}^{2}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2-5{x}^{2}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =1+(2-5{x}^{2})\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1+(2-5{x}^{2})\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =0+(2-5{x}^{2})^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+(2-5{x}^{2})\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(-10x)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x+(2-5{x}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =-10x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{2-5{x}^{2}}{x} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =-10x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-5x+\\frac{2}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(-10x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-5x+\\frac{2}{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=e{x}^{2-5{x}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =e{x}^{2-5{x}^{2}}\\cdot (-10x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-5x+\\frac{2}{x}) \\\\  & =e{x}^{1-5{x}^{2}}\\cdot x(-10x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-5x+\\frac{2}{x}) \\\\  & =e{x}^{1-5{x}^{2}}\\cdot (-10{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-5{x}^{2}+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504703 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064306 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "44425e0c-c9f0-5589-a298-071f456fd0c5" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating X Raised to the Power of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5de69524-b32f-529b-907d-70d9251c90c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\ln x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504194}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5de69524-b32f-529b-907d-70d9251c90c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\ln x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504194} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cbd0b0a-4f04-5099-bf6e-7a48a06d53ff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504543}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e9eb4864-b21d-5e66-84e3-4be25804889c" :question/math-academy-id "q-341713" :question/problem "Given that $y=5e{x}^{1/x}$, find ${y}^{′}.$\n\n${y}^{′}=5e{x}^{1/x-2}\\cdot ($\n{{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=5e{x}^{1/x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5e{x}^{1/x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5e)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{1/x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5e)+(\\frac{1}{x})\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5e)+(\\frac{1}{x})\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =0+{(\\frac{1}{x})}^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+(\\frac{1}{x})\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+(\\frac{1}{x})\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & ={x}^{-2}(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y[{x}^{-2}(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=5e{x}^{1/x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =5e{x}^{1/x}[{x}^{-2}(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)] \\\\  & =5e{x}^{1/x-2}\\cdot (1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504692 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064306 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "44425e0c-c9f0-5589-a298-071f456fd0c5" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating X Raised to the Power of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "016328d0-e890-5f0f-acd5-5481d9b4f783" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+x\\tan x\\ln x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504033}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "016328d0-e890-5f0f-acd5-5481d9b4f783" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+x\\tan x\\ln x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504033} :answer-field/id #uuid "a32a9416-0150-598e-ac86-6202b9e0f123" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504550}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d597eea3-7247-5548-9071-6c8d8bbf677d" :question/math-academy-id "q-341717" :question/problem "Given that $y={x}^{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\cdot ($\n{{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x(\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y={x}^{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}{x}) \\\\  & ={x}^{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}{x}\\cdot (x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+1) \\\\  & ={x}^{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\cdot (x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504657 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064307 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6cc92652-6746-583d-bb0e-53991d5f5e1d" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Function Raised to the Power of X"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19cbcd6f-25b2-5cce-957b-c04f39806845" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5x^2+2x)\\ln(5x^2+2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504078}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19cbcd6f-25b2-5cce-957b-c04f39806845" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5x^2+2x)\\ln(5x^2+2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504078} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5ec8274-52d3-526d-accb-2a89c9a62542" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504589}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9798e200-13a6-549b-86eb-d44d6cb98aeb" :question/math-academy-id "q-341719" :question/problem "Given that $y=(5{x}^{2}+2x)^{x}$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(5{x}^{2}+2x)^{x-1}\\cdot ($\n{{field-1}}$+10{x}^{2}+2x)$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=(5{x}^{2}+2x)^{x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((5{x}^{2}+2x)^{x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5{x}^{2}+2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5{x}^{2}+2x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5{x}^{2}+2x))+(x)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5{x}^{2}+2x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(5{x}^{2}+2x)+x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{5{x}^{2}+2x}\\cdot (10x+2)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5{x}^{2}+2x)+\\frac{10{x}^{2}+2x}{5{x}^{2}+2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5{x}^{2}+2x)+\\frac{10{x}^{2}+2x}{5{x}^{2}+2x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=(5{x}^{2}+2x)^{x}:$    \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(5{x}^{2}+2x)^{x}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5{x}^{2}+2x)+\\frac{10{x}^{2}+2x}{5{x}^{2}+2x}) \\\\  & =(5{x}^{2}+2x)^{x}\\cdot (\\frac{(5{x}^{2}+2x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5{x}^{2}+2x)}{5{x}^{2}+2x}+\\frac{10{x}^{2}+2x}{5{x}^{2}+2x}) \\\\  & =(5{x}^{2}+2x)^{x-1}\\cdot ((5{x}^{2}+2x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5{x}^{2}+2x)+10{x}^{2}+2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504656 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064308 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "94a2b8cb-aca4-5e18-bb5c-71c0738c9e30" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Function Raised to the Power of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b71b8dc8-607f-5822-afac-e0d10c462052" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin(x)\\tan(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504349}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b71b8dc8-607f-5822-afac-e0d10c462052" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin(x)\\tan(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504349} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1418ec4-9e2c-5aec-b01d-aff904f686c2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504581}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "965ab36a-7265-52be-bed3-0fcb25681407" :question/math-academy-id "q-341907" :question/problem "Given that $y=(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x))^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}$, find ${y}^{′}.$\n\n${y}^{′}=(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x))^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x))\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x))^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x))^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x))) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)))+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x))+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x))+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x))+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x))^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x))+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x))\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413628 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064308 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "94a2b8cb-aca4-5e18-bb5c-71c0738c9e30" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Function Raised to the Power of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e24a86b9-7405-5dbf-acfb-1af04c6cd34f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413617}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e24a86b9-7405-5dbf-acfb-1af04c6cd34f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413617} :answer-field/id #uuid "76f8ee9f-a89b-56b6-9a08-fe333af36907" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413623}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6d2d4364-ca99-526d-979f-b7f7eb9eac46" :question/math-academy-id "q-341945" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent line to the curve $y={(\\sqrt{2x+1})}^{{x}^{2}+1}$ at $x=0.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=0$ equals the function's derivative at the corresponding point.\n\nWe have the function \n\n$$\ny={(\\sqrt{2x+1})}^{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({(\\sqrt{2x+1})}^{{x}^{2}+1}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =({x}^{2}+1)\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2x+1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\,(({x}^{2}+1)\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2x+1})) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\,{y}^{′} & =({x}^{2}+1)^{′}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2x+1})+({x}^{2}+1)\\,{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2x+1}))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\,{y}^{′} & =2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2x+1})+({x}^{2}+1)\\,(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x+1}}\\cdot \\frac{2}{2\\sqrt{2x+1}}) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)+\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{2x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y\\,(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)+\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{2x+1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y={(\\sqrt{2x+1})}^{{x}^{2}+1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={(\\sqrt{2x+1})}^{{x}^{2}+1}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)+\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{2x+1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of $y={(\\sqrt{2x+1})}^{{x}^{2}+1}$ at $x=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={y}^{′}{|}_{x=0} & ={(\\sqrt{2(0)+1})}^{(0)^{2}+1}(0\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2(0)+1)+\\frac{(0)^{2}+1}{2(0)+1}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (0\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504637 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064308 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "94a2b8cb-aca4-5e18-bb5c-71c0738c9e30" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Function Raised to the Power of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bdcbb96-5ec9-5007-b256-214fc5f8397c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504308}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9bdcbb96-5ec9-5007-b256-214fc5f8397c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504308} :answer-field/id #uuid "36d45e39-f71d-51b2-ae6d-2faee3a34b79" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504499}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "650ff22d-86ac-5784-9e2d-04a2cd40b0db" :question/math-academy-id "q-341951" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent line to the curve $y=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{{x}^{2}}$ at $x=\\frac{e}{3}.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=\\frac{e}{3}$ equals the function's derivative at the corresponding point.\n\nWe have the function \n\n$$\ny=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{{x}^{2}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & ={x}^{2}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =({x}^{2})^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)))+({x}^{2})\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =2x\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))+{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))+\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))+\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{{x}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{{x}^{2}}(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))+\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of $y=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{{x}^{2}}$ at $x=\\frac{e}{3}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={y}^{′}{|}_{x=e/3} & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e))^{(e/3)^{2}}(2(\\frac{e}{3})\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e))+\\frac{(\\frac{e}{3})}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)}) \\\\  & =(1)^{(e/3)^{2}}(2(\\frac{e}{3})\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+\\frac{(\\frac{e}{3})}{1}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (0+\\frac{e}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
